<section class="section tech section--ink" id="what" aria-labelledby="what-title">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="head " data-reveal><p class="kicker">What it does</p><h2 id="what-title">Takes On the Drudgery. Leaves the Music to <em>You</em>.</h2></div><div class="feature-list"><article class="feature" data-reveal><h3>From words to a session</h3><p>Plans the sections, key, tempo and instruments, then writes the parts and builds the project with named tracks, markers and a starting mix.</p></article><article class="feature" data-reveal><h3>From a score to a song</h3><p>Reads a score as a PDF, a photo or MusicXML, keeps the melody note for note, fits chords to it and arranges a band around it, following the score’s own bar lengths and repeats.</p></article><article class="feature" data-reveal><h3>Learns from the composer’s templates</h3><p>Has studied the layouts of about 190 of the composer’s own Cubase and Logic templates (folders, buses, effect returns, sends and levels), lays out each new session the way he would, and explains every choice in one plain line.</p></article><article class="feature" data-reveal><h3>A music brain</h3><p>Learned song forms, energy curves, transitions and instrument entries shape each arrangement, and the composer’s own words, such as “quiet intro” or “start with the mukhda”, always win.</p></article><article class="feature" data-reveal><h3>Mix and master</h3><p>Sets a starting mix on the composer’s own arrangements and masters through WaveLab.</p></article><article class="feature feature--dev" data-reveal><h3>Dorico loop</h3><p>Sends a player list to Dorico, and places the Dorico score back onto the template tracks in any of the DAWs.</p><span class="feature__tag">In development</span></article></div>
</div>
</section>
